  "file.c2_paper12.html": "<div style=\"text-align:center;border-bottom:3px double #000;padding-bottom:6px\"><b style=\"font-size:28px;letter-spacing:12px\">Wutong</b><br>Wucheng No. 7 High Student Newspaper · December 1999 (Issue 41)<br><span style=\"font-size:12px\">School Youth League · Student editors: Song Zhiyuan, Su Rui · Layout: Xu Jia</span></div>\n<h3>Millennium celebration on the evening of December 31</h3>\n<p>On the final day of term, the school Youth League will present “Welcome the New Millennium” in the auditorium, with over ten acts. Zhou Ting of Senior Three (2) will close the show with a solo. Afterward, all teachers and students will gather on the sports field for the midnight countdown. Follow teachers’ directions and stay safe.</p>\n<h3>What to do about the Millennium Bug?</h3>\n<p style=\"font-size:13px\">Xu Jia · Senior Three (2)</p>\n<p>Early computers saved space by storing only the last two digits of a year: 1999 became 99. In 2000, adding one makes 00, which some programs read as 1900. This is the Millennium Bug.<br>It is not a virus, is not contagious, and will not make lifts fall. It affects programs that calculate by year: interest, ages, expiry dates and sorting.<br>The school computers are being patched in batches this December. See the Computers board on the BBS. Bring a floppy disk to the lab to copy a patch for your home machine.</p>\n<h3>[Public information] Wucheng telephone numbers gain an eighth digit on New Year’s Day</h3>\n<div class=\"scan\" style=\"padding:8px;font-size:13px\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><b>Wucheng Telecommunications Bureau Notice</b>(No. 99-27)</p>\n<p>With approval from the provincial Posts and Telecommunications Administration, Wucheng local telephone numbers will change at  <b>00:00 on January 1, 2000</b> from seven to eight digits. The arrangements are as follows:</p>\n<p>1. Conversion rules<br>\n(1) Prefix numbers beginning with 2, 3 or 4 with 8. Example: 231 5678 becomes 8231 5678.<br>\n(2) Prefix numbers beginning with 5 or 6 with 6. Example: 562 0011 becomes 6562 0011.<br>\n(3) Prefix numbers beginning with 7 or 8 (East Lake District and Wucheng Development Zone) with 2. Example: 712 3456 becomes 2712 3456.<br>\n(4) Exchanges 340–349 under the West City branch (Xinhua Road, Wutong Road and Xuefu Street) were moved to the new West City exchange in November 1999 and <b>are exempt from rule (1)</b>: prefix all these numbers with 6. Example: 342 1100 becomes 6342 1100.</p>\n<p>2. Special service numbers (110, 119, 120, 122, 112, 114, etc.) and paging station numbers are unchanged.</p>\n<p>3. Public telephones, including IC-card and coin-operated phones, change at the same time under the same rules.</p>\n<p>4. The transition period runs from January 1 to March 31, 2000. Calling an old number will play a change-of-number message. New numbers are in the Wucheng 2000 Telephone Directory, sold at service offices and also available on CD-ROM.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">Wucheng Telecommunications Bureau · December 10, 1999</p>\n</div>\n<p>(The school switchboard changes from 345 1688 to 6345 1688. See the directory for other numbers.)</p>\n<h3>College entrance exam countdown: 206 days</h3>\n<p>Senior Three final exams begin January 17. Winter holiday extra classes begin February 10; details will follow.</p>",
  "file.c2_paper01.html": "<div style=\"text-align:center;border-bottom:3px double #000;padding-bottom:6px\"><b style=\"font-size:28px;letter-spacing:12px\">Wutong</b><br>Wucheng No. 7 High Student Newspaper · January 2000 (Issue 42)<br><span style=\"font-size:12px\">School Youth League · Student editors: Song Zhiyuan, Su Rui · Layout: Xu Jia</span></div>\n<p style=\"font-size:12px;text-align:center\">Cover: Senior Three (2) on the sports field on Millennium Eve (photograph: school photo group, Senior One (3))</p>\n<h3>Hello, 2000! Our millennium celebration concludes</h3>\n<p>At 19:00 on December 31, “Welcome the New Millennium” opened in the school auditorium. The opening dance “Light of the Millennium” was full of energy. Senior Two (1)’s comic dialogue “The Millennium Bug and Me” portrayed the bug as a tiny insect afraid of the cold, to much laughter. Senior Three (2)’s sketch “College Entrance Exam Countdown” struck a chord with the final-year students.</p>\n<p>Su Rui of Senior Three (2) and Cheng Yifan of Senior Two (1) hosted the event. Zhou Ting of Senior Three (2) closed with the solo “Waiting for You Until Midnight.” Her clear voice drew long applause.</p>\n<p>At 23:30, the celebration ended and everyone moved to the sports field. Mr. Gu Qiubai led the countdown. “Ten, nine, eight…” With the great clock’s chimes, 2000 arrived. Fireworks rose; students embraced and made wishes.</p>\n<h3>Computer lab and server enter the new year smoothly</h3>\n<p>The computer teaching group reports that, following December’s remediation, the lab and BBS server entered 2000 at midnight without abnormalities.</p>\n<p class=\"faded\" style=\"font-size:12px\">Also reported: in the early hours of New Year’s Day, a female Senior Three student died in an accident on campus. The school expresses deep sorrow and has strengthened safety measures. Students must not enter dangerous areas such as the water tower.</p>\n<h3>Final exam arrangements</h3>\n<p>Senior Three: January 17–19. Senior One and Two: January 19–21.</p>",
